B-28 [promoted → receipt-worker-correctness] `receipt/worker.go`'s `parseEventDate` stamps a COGS period from server-local time, outside the app timezone — `backend/internal/receipt/worker.go:1056` ends `return time.Now().Format("2006-01-02")`: when Mercury's `CreatedAt` matches none of the accepted layouts, the fallback is the **server's** local date (UTC in the container), and that value is written to `pending_purchases.event_date` — a **COGS period assignment**, and the one field that WINS the `COALESCE` in `pendingPeriodDateExpr`. So the single path that is exempt from card A1's zone unification is the path that produces the value the zone would otherwise decide: a receipt ingested between 20:00 New York and midnight UTC is filed to tomorrow's period, permanently, with no boundary for A1 to move. Bounded by how often Mercury emits an unparseable `CreatedAt` — which is unmeasured, and silent when it happens (no log, no `reason`). (flagged in the `overnight-20260729-2` park note for card A1; carried out of the 2026-08-01 resume as explicitly out of footprint (`backend/internal/receipt` is not in A1's packages))
B-29 [done — disclosed + finalised 2026-09-01 as docs/contracts/NOTICE-sales-processor-2026-09-01.md (ledger T-48); §1 exposure window checked 2026-08-04, no past figure restated] HQ changed what `/period-summary`'s completeness gate returns on 2026-06-06 and never told sales-processor — an undisclosed two-repo contract drift on the payroll gate — Phase 21 published `completeness.pending_review_ids` as `pending_purchases.id` rows where `(created_at AT TIME ZONE 'America/Chicago')::date BETWEEN from AND to` (`21-SALES-PROCESSOR-CONTRACT.md:67`, archived in `875e26c` 2026-06-05), and that was **accurate to the code as it then stood**. On **2026-06-06** commit `cf959bd` — quick task `260606-0gh` (`.planning/quick/260606-0gh-completeness-gate-filters-pending-review/`), a task entirely separate from Phase 21 — replaced it with `COALESCE(event_date, (created_at AT TIME ZONE 'America/Chicago')::date)` **and did not touch the contract**. That task's own PLAN.md reasons explicitly about *"a sales-processor query for May 25–31"*, so the counterparty impact was in view at the time. **It is a behaviour change, not a clarification:** under the published expression a late-discovered receipt (the PLAN's own example — a May 29 purchase ingested June 2) was filtered on `created_at`, fell outside the May window, and did **NOT** block May payroll; under the shipped code its extracted `event_date` puts it inside the window and it **does**. So sales-processor may have been receiving an undocumented `ready:false` — a **blocked payroll run** — since June 2026, with no way to reconcile it against the contract it was handed. Card A1's `:67` amendment initially compounded this by asserting *"the `COALESCE` is not new"* / "carried since Phase 21", which reads to the counterparty as *"you were always getting this"* — the opposite of the truth; that text is corrected on `card/a1-app-timezone-unify-new-york`. 🛑 **This is a SECOND notice to sales-processor, independent of A1's timezone notice.** The operator decides what is told and when; A1's contract edit states the drift on the page but does not deliver it. (adversarial review of card A1 on run `overnight-20260801`, finding F1 — verified at source against `875e26c` and `cf959bd`)
B-33 [dropped — night-crew tooling defect, tracked clone-side as B-346/B-347] The run-evidence oracle cannot see ANY hq slate run's closing evidence — every night reads `no-run-evidence` at the next launch — `night-crew run-evidence check --repo . --run 20260801` returns **`no-run-evidence`** on a night that landed 4 of 4 with a committed closeout record, a full conflict log, HANDOFF and DECISIONS-NEEDED. Two independent path mismatches, either of which alone is sufficient: **(1)** the oracle reads `.night-crew/runs/<runid>/{summary.json,metrics.jsonl,journal.jsonl}` — i.e. `runs/20260801/` — but the launch prompt mandates the run directory be `.night-crew/runs/2026-08-01-autonomous/`, so those three can never be found regardless of whether they are written; **(2)** it reads `reference/conflicts-<runid>.md` relative to the **repo root**, and **hq has no root `reference/`** — this repo's reference home is `.night-crew/knowledge/reference/`, which is where the conflict log, the closeout record, the slate and the launch prompt all correctly live. `night-crew launch-prompt` searches BOTH homes and resolves correctly; `run-evidence check` does not, so the two verbs disagree about where this repo keeps its artifacts. **Consequence:** §1 of `/nc-run`'s "has this slate already run?" guard is **inert in every night-crew target repo scaffolded this way**. The next launch will read a completed night as never having run and offer to execute the slate again. The §2 run-branch guard is the only thing that catches it, and only while the run branch stays unmerged — once triage merges `overnight-20260801` to `dev`, **nothing** stops a re-execution of an already-landed slate. (overnight-20260801 closeout; the closeout record was written per `/nc-run` §3a and the oracle still could not see it)
B-35 [promoted → gate-harness-honesty (roadmap round 2026-08-05)] The standard gate command `go test ./...` DROPS a database it does not own — a B-16 trigger sitting inside the ladder itself — `TestRowVisibilityRLS` drops and recreates its FDW fixture database on entry, and its default name is `hq_test_b2_fdw`. Any plain `go test ./...` in this repo therefore destroys `hq_test_b2_fdw` without asking, including a reviewer's or a concurrently-running card's. Reproduced at morning triage: the adversarial reviewer's first full `go test -p 1 -count=1 ./...` run silently dropped it before the reviewer switched to `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB=hq_adv_b2_fdw` for every subsequent probe. **This is exactly B-16's failure mode** — one agent destroying another's database, where the victim sees a broken suite rather than a stolen fixture — except that B-16 was about *reviewer cleanup* and this is baked into the **primary gate command every card runs**. Concurrent dispatch (three tracks, this project's normal overnight shape) makes collision the expected case, not the unlucky one. (morning triage 2026-07-31; found by the adversarial re-execution of run `overnight-20260801`, disclosed by that agent against itself and not reported by the run)
B-36 [promoted → gate-rls-skip-is-not-pass (roadmap round 2026-08-05)] `internal/sync` prints `ok` and exits 0 while skipping B2's ENTIRE RLS attack suite — the row-visibility security gate cannot be distinguished from a gate that never ran — `resolveSpikeConfig` locates the sync substrate by shelling out to `docker compose -p spike-supabase … port`. **Any** reason that command fails — docker absent, compose project down, ports not published, a different project name — is treated as "not configured" and becomes `t.Skip`, so the package reports `ok` and the ladder's recorded line **"G2 Go … 9 packages `ok`, 0 failed"** carries **zero** information about whether the security suite executed. Verified at triage by execution: with `PATH` stripped of docker, `go test -run TestRowVisibilityRLS ./internal/sync/` exits **0** with `ok`, and only `-v` reveals `--- SKIP` and the test's own honest line *"no sync substrate configured — skipping, and SKIPPED IS NOT PASSED"*. The test says the right thing; **nothing in the gate reads it.** 🛑 Distinct from **B-22**, which is about `verify-test-harness.sh` Check B aggregating with OR — that is the harness script; this is the suite's own skip path, and `verify-test-harness.sh` does not cover this axis at all (it grades only the `DB_TEST_URL` case). This is the repo's characteristic bug class — a check whose subject set can go empty — inside the card that exists to prove row visibility. (morning triage 2026-07-31; reproduced by the adversarial re-execution of run `overnight-20260801`, finding F-1)
B-37 [promoted → P1 `build-deploy-manifest-integrity` (slate-20260802). **✅ LANDED 2026-08-01 on `card/p1-build-deploy-manifest-integrity` (`fc768ff` guard, `9ea937a` the fix it demanded, `4c455ff` the guard on the guard), run `overnight-20260802`** — `build-sw.js` now runs `importReachabilityTransform` as the LAST `manifestTransform` and **throws**, so `sw.js` is never written on a failure rather than written-then-complained-about. **Red reproduced first, at exit 0:** with `sync-rxdb/bootstrap.js` dropped from HEAD and left in the working tree, the pre-fix `build-sw.js` printed the skip, emitted **28 files / 2132.6 KB**, and exited **0**, writing an `sw.js` that precached `workflows.html` without its module entry point. The guard exits **1** on the identical case and an unreferenced scratch `*.html` still exits **0** — the invariant is reachability, not completeness, exactly as this lead specifies. 🛑 **It then found the defect live on the merged tree with no synthetic case at all: `log.js` (all 7 precached pages) and `tab.js` (5) were referenced everywhere and in `globPatterns` nowhere**, since the ad-hoc commits that added them — `log.js` at `1ec8725` (2026-04-21), `tab.js` a week later at `cfe7edc` (2026-04-28), each first referenced in the same commit that introduced it. They ship via `COPY *.html *.js` so they have always worked online and always failed offline — and `tab.js` applies `#tab=N` before paint, so five of seven tools opened on a returning offline client with every tab section visible at once and no switching. **Precache count 29 → 31 (+2.0 KB); 31 is the new invariant.** Guard integrity: 18 files parsed, 30 references resolved, three anti-vacuity checks (zero files / zero refs / two canary edges), 7 tests in `tests/sw-manifest.spec.js`, and **4 mutations each red exactly one of them** (drop the transform → the end-to-end test; collapse the bare-specifier rule → the `ws` test *and* the real build; null the vacuity checks → the untrustworthy test; warn instead of throw → the end-to-end test).] `build-sw.js` exits 0 while silently dropping shipping assets from the precache — warnings only, no failure — reproduced at triage: with git HEAD at a pre-bundle commit and the tip's assets present in the working tree, `node build-sw.js` printed `skipped (not in HEAD): vendor/rxdb.bundle.js` plus six `sync-rxdb/` and `sync-schema/` modules, emitted **22 files / 1481.9 KB instead of 29 / 2111.1 KB**, and **exited 0**. `workflows.html` IS precached and loads `sync-rxdb/bootstrap.js` as a module, so the shipped service worker would cache a page whose dependencies it deliberately omitted — Obligation 5's production-outage mode. It bit for real during run `overnight-20260801`'s merge 3, where regenerating mid-merge produced a 24-file manifest; the run caught it and recorded the ordering rule in the conflict log, but **the rule is procedural and nothing mechanical enforces it**. 🛑 Distinct from **B-17**, which covers the wrong `--name-only` C-quoting comment and the HEAD-vs-`origin/main` residual — neither of those is the exit code. Reading from git HEAD is by design (card `precache-manifest-from-head`); exiting 0 after dropping a file another precached file imports is not. (morning triage 2026-07-31; reproduced by the adversarial re-execution of run `overnight-20260801`, finding F-2)
B-42 [**destination: `sync-hard-cutover`** — that card owns the write path and is the moment `HQ_SYNC_REST_URL` is first set in a real deploy, which is the first moment the live leg carries anything at all. Until then the stream is connected to a door that answers 503. Lead: three options in ascending cost — (i) apply the single-clause Realtime `filter` to the three collections where it is expressible and accept `responses`; (ii) reshape the `responses` scope so one clause suffices (e.g. a queryable checklist key on the row, which is a SCHEMA change and therefore a different card's); (iii) fork the plugin's live subscription. 🛑 Do not treat this as reopening C-2: the preference is about pulling a collection whole, and after A1 nothing is pulled whole.] `SYNC-REALTIME-SCOPE` — the pull is scoped per checklist but the live `postgres_changes` stream is not, so an out-of-scope row still reaches the phone when someone changes it — 🛑 **RENUMBERED FROM `B-39` during the G6 fix round of run `20260802`: three legs that night independently claimed B-39, and the pre-step's B-39/B-40/B-41 were already merged to the run branch and keep those numbers. Content and destination are unchanged.** Card `sync-replication-scope-per-checklist` (run `20260802`, A1) gave every replicated collection a `pull.queryBuilder`, which is the only scoping seam `rxdb/plugins/replication-supabase` offers. It scopes the PULL. It does not scope the LIVE leg: the plugin opens `client.channel(replicationIdentifier).on('postgres_changes', {event:'*', schema:'public', table}, …)` with **no `filter` key**, and feeds every event it receives straight into the local store as a master change. So a `submission_responses` row belonging to a checklist this device never opened, **edited while the page is open**, still lands in IndexedDB — the pull scope does not evict it and nothing re-checks it. **Two seams were examined and both are genuinely closed, which is why this is a backlog item and not a missed line of code.** (a) `pull.modifier` DOES reach stream documents — RxDB's `masterChangeStream$` does `documents.map(m => modifier(m))` — but it cannot DROP one: there is no null filter on the downstream path (the push path has one, `newDocumentState===null?null:`; the pull path does not), so returning null injects a null document rather than skipping a row. (b) Realtime's own `postgres_changes` `filter` option accepts a **single** `column=op.value` clause, which can express `checklists`/`approvals`/`templates` but **cannot** express the `responses` scope, which is `or(submission_id.eq.X, and(submission_id.is.null, field_id.in.(…)))` — and `responses` is the unbounded collection the whole card is about. A partial fix that filters three collections and leaves the fourth is worth having but is a design call, not a mechanical one. **Bounded, and stated so the size is not overestimated:** the pull is the unbounded leg (all history, every page load); the stream is bounded by what other people change while this crew member is looking at their phone. The card's ~23 s figure is on the pull and the pull is now scoped. 🛑 **The unbounded-phone-storage consequence is IMPROVED, NOT FIXED — reworded during the G6 fix round (finding F-6), which caught the original wording claiming otherwise.** RxDB's downstream only adds; nothing evicts, and there is no retention sweep for the four replicated collections. A phone still accumulates every checklist it has **opened** — the bound moved from *all history* to *opened checklists*, which is a large improvement and is not the same as bounded. · _run `20260802`, card A1 `sync-replication-scope-per-checklist`, found while measuring the vendored plugin's pull construction against `vendor/rxdb.bundle.js`; recorded in that file's `REPLICATION SCOPE` header block and in `sync-rxdb/client.js` rather than only here_ (new)
B-43 [**partial ruling 2026-08-07 (ledger T-43, slate 20260808-2):** Approvals stays on re-fetch (recorded); the My Checklists read path is deliberately kept OPEN by the operator — no card may decide it; entry stays open on that named remainder only] The replication scope model has no shape for HQ's two ENTRY POINTS — "My Checklists" and "Approvals" are LISTS over many submissions, and the scope is singular by construction — Card `sync-replication-scope-per-checklist` (run `20260802`, A1) made `scope.checklistId` **mandatory and singular** and scoped `checklists` to `id.eq.<one>`, `approvals` to `submission_id.eq.<one>`, and `responses` to that one submission plus its own field ids. That is exactly right for the checklist-fill view, which is the view preference `architecture/C-2` names. **But it is not the view a crew member lands on.** `workflows.html`'s 3-tab layout opens on **My Checklists**, a list of every submission assigned to this user, and its second tab is **Approvals**, a list of every rejection awaiting this user — both are lists over MANY submissions, and neither can name a single `checklistId`. So under `sync-hard-cutover` there are only two outcomes and nobody has chosen between them: either **those two pages cannot be RxDB-backed at all** (they stay on the REST path, and the cutover is partial by design rather than by accident), or **someone invents a widening** at the moment the page loads — which is a C-2 widening and therefore needs a recorded decision, taken deliberately rather than discovered while wiring. · _run `20260802`, G6 adversarial review of card A1 (finding F-7), confirmed against `sync-rxdb/client.js`'s `normalizeScope` (throws without a single `checklistId`) and `scopeFilterFor` (`checklists` → `id.eq`)_ · promoted → activate-list-views-or-state-they-stay-rest (roadmap round 2026-08-05) (**destination: `sync-hard-cutover`** — that card owns the write path and the page wiring, and is the first moment any page actually calls `startHQReplication`. 🛑 **This is NOT the same gap as `B-42 SYNC-REALTIME-SCOPE`**: B-42 is about the live `postgres_changes` leg being unscoped; this one is about the PULL scope having no expressible shape for a list view. B-42 could be fixed completely and this would still be open. Lead: three options in ascending cost — (i) leave the two list views on the existing REST endpoints and scope RxDB to the fill view only, stating it (cheapest, and arguably what C-2 already implies since a list needs summaries, not every response row); (ii) admit a list scope — `checklists: assigned_to.eq.<userId> AND status.in.(…)` plus a date floor — as an explicitly recorded C-2 widening with a bound that is not "all history"; (iii) a summary projection/view the list reads instead, which is a SCHEMA change and therefore a different card's.)
