B-46 [**destination: next milestone**] With no token the launcher paints NOTHING — no message, no spinner, no affordance, permanently — `index.html`'s fail-closed identity branch is correct about what it refuses (it must not paint the previous user's tiles), but it has no visual half. With no verified `/api/v1/me` and no device identity token, `readCachedApps(deviceId)` returns `null` because `deviceId` is null, `filterTilesByPermissions` is therefore never called, and `.grid` stays at the inline `visibility:hidden` it ships with. The crew member sees the wordmark and empty space. There is no "you're offline", no "sign in to load your tools", no retry — and nothing on screen distinguishes it from a broken app. It **self-heals** on the next `index.html` load that reaches the network, which is why it is not blocking and why it did not surface as a defect during the card. 🛑 It is nonetheless the ordinary first-launch-offline experience on a truck. · _run `overnight-20260802`, card B1 G6 adversarial review, NON-BLOCKING-6; filed rather than built under the run's standing scope freeze — a UI for this is a design decision, not a fix round's call_ (new)
B-49 [**destination: `sync-hard-cutover`** — the same destination B-42 already has, and for the same reason: that card owns the write path and is the first moment `HQ_SYNC_REST_URL` is set in a real deploy, which is the first moment any live leg carries anything. Lead: fold into B-42's option (i) — `approvals` is single-clause expressible, so it costs nothing extra to include when that fix is made. Do NOT file this as a separate fix; it is a line item on B-42's ticket.] `submission_rejections` just became the FOURTH collection B-42's unscoped live leg applies to — and it is the one that has never carried a single event — Card `sync-rxdb-write-policies` (run `20260802`, A2) gives `submission_rejections` a SELECT policy for the first time (ledger T-30 decision 111 consequence 1). The table has been in the `supabase_realtime` publication with `REPLICA IDENTITY FULL` since `sync-schema/sql/0001_sync_tables.sql` shipped it, but with **zero policies it was deny-all**, so Realtime evaluated RLS against every row and delivered nothing — the subscription existed and was inert. It is no longer inert. **B-42 (`SYNC-REALTIME-SCOPE`) was written and scoped against three collections and now applies to four**, and the fourth is the approvals stream, which is the one a crew member's phone is most likely to receive while looking at a different checklist. 🛑 **This is a widening of B-42's blast radius, not a new mechanism** — the seam analysis in B-42 (Realtime's `filter` option accepts a single `column=op.value` clause; `pull.modifier` cannot drop a row) is unchanged and applies verbatim. `approvals` IS expressible as a single clause (`submission_id=eq.X`), so it falls in B-42's option (i) bucket rather than the hard `responses` bucket. Bounded the same way B-42 is: the live leg carries only what other people change while this crew member is looking at their phone. · _run `20260802`, card A2 `sync-rxdb-write-policies`, found while writing `0004_write_policies.sql` §5d — noticed because 0001 enrols the table in the publication and 0003 §5d explains at length why it has no policy, and the two facts only compose once the policy exists. Filed as B-46 and renumbered to B-49 mid-run: the B1 leg merged first and kept B-46, and B-50 was reserved for the substrate-isolation finding. The renumbering note previously sat inside the handle itself, which made the entry unparseable to `night-crew backlog check` — moved here at triage 2026-08-02_ (new)
B-47 [**destination: NEXT milestone (substrate operability).** Lead: cheapest is a `sync-schema/apply.sh` that globs `sql/*.sql | sort` and applies each inside a transaction, plus a one-row `hq_sync_schema_version` table so a substrate can be asked. A test asserting the glob order matches what `rvConnect` applies would close the drift between the two lists without inventing a runner at all, and is worth doing first.] `sync-schema/sql/` has no applier, no manifest and no ordering test — the only thing that applies the four files in order is a hard-coded list inside a Go test — `sync-schema/sql/` now holds four files (`0001_sync_tables`, `0002_hq_fdw`, `0003_rls_policies`, `0004_write_policies`) that MUST be applied in that order: 0003's policies call `hq_jwt_claim` which 0003 defines, 0004's `hq_can_approve_field` calls `hq_can_see_field` which 0003 defines, and both depend on foreign tables 0002 creates. There is **no goose, no migration table, no numbered-runner script and nothing that records what a given substrate has had applied.** The ordering exists in exactly two places, both of them incidental: the filename prefixes (a convention, unenforced) and `rvConnect` in `backend/internal/sync/rowvisibility_rls_test.go`, which names all four paths in a fixed sequence. 🛑 **The consequence is that adding `0005_*.sql` requires editing a Go test file to make it apply at all**, and a substrate applied by hand in the wrong order fails with a `function does not exist` error inside a policy at request time — 0002's own header calls that failure mode out for the user mapping and it is the same shape here. Not urgent while nothing is deployed (`HQ_SYNC_REST_URL` is unset everywhere) and while the only consumer is a test that hard-codes the list. It becomes urgent the moment a second environment exists, because there will then be no way to ask a substrate which files it has. · _run `20260802`, card A2 `sync-rxdb-write-policies`, found while adding the fourth file and having to e