B-51 [**destination: `sync-hard-cutover`** — it owns the write path and is the first card that sets `HQ_SYNC_REST_URL` in a real deploy, which is the first moment a stray DELETE privilege can be reached by anything but a test. Lead: put the revoke in **0001**, next to the grant whose wording implied it, so it applies in every mode including both reds and W12's grant assertion can be a hard one. While there: `TRUNCATE` and `REFERENCES` are also held and also unwanted.] The "two independent gates refuse a hard DELETE" claim was measurably false — there is ONE gate, because Supabase's default privileges already granted `authenticated` ALL and a `grant` revokes nothing — `sync-schema/sql/0001_sync_tables.sql` carries `grant select, insert, update on … to authenticated` on all four replicated tables, and both `0004_write_policies.sql` §1(b) and variant W12's comment read that as a privilege gate standing independently of the absent DELETE policy. **It never did.** A `GRANT` is additive; Supabase's own `alter default privileges` had already handed `authenticated` ALL on every table in `public` before 0001 ran. MEASURED on the live substrate, identically on all four tables: `authenticated` holds `DELETE, INSERT, REFERENCES, SELECT, TRIGGER, TRUNCATE, UPDATE` — and under `SYNC_RLS_SKIP_POLICIES=1` all three of W12's DELETEs returned **HTTP 204 with the rows gone**. 🛑 **Behaviour is green today** — the one real gate, RLS enabled with no DELETE policy, is deny-all and holds — **but the defense-in-depth was imaginary and two files stated it as fact.** The prose in both is now corrected and W12 logs the measured grant set rather than asserting a gate it does not have. The fix that would make the second gate real is one statement: `revoke delete, truncate on public.{checklist_templates,checklist_submissions,submission_responses,submission_rejections} from authenticated`. It was NOT applied by the fix round that found it, for two reasons worth carrying: the grants live in **0001,