B-50 [🛑 **aggravated 2026-08-28 (A3 re-gate):** Activity 5 made the shared substrate PERSISTENT — `task sync:dev:up` and the suites' default resolution name the same `spike-supabase` project — so residue is now permanent by design: `TestJWTBridgeRLS` reds on `spikec-*` dev rows on ANY tree, and the blamed card can never be the cause. Same tree green against an isolated `-p spike-regate` stack via `SPIKE_DB_URL`/`SPIKE_REST_URL`, so lead (i) works by hand today; the defect is that the DEFAULT resolves the dev stack] `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB` isolates only the HQ-side FDW source database — the Supabase `public` schema and the single PostgREST have NO isolation variable, so one leg's policies red another leg's suite — B-35's rule ("every leg MUST set a unique `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB`, `TEST_PORT` and DB prefix") was followed to the letter by every leg on run `20260802`, and it was **not sufficient**. `rvHQDatabase()` moves only the throwaway HQ database that the FDW reads *from*. The substrate the policies actually live on — `spike-supabase-db-1`'s `public` schema — and the single PostgREST in front of it are **shared by every concurrent leg with no override at all**. 🛑 **Observed, not theorised:** card B1's fix round hit `TestRowVisibilityRLS/V18` FAILING in a worktree whose entire diff touches **zero** files under `backend/` and **zero** under `sync-schema/`. Cause: the concurrently-running A2 leg had created `submission_rejections_select/_insert/_update` on the shared substrate, while B1's tree still carried the pre-rewrite V18 asserting those policies are absent. It reproduced with the substrate otherwise quiet, so it was persisted residue rather than a live race. **This is strictly worse than B-35 in one respect: it reds cards that touch no Go whatsoever, so the card that gets blamed is never the card that caused it.** B1 correctly refused to "fix" it — dropping another leg's policies mid-run is the B-16 incident, where a dropped test database read as a passing suite. A2's fix round removed the *residue* half by registering a `t.Cleanup` so `tearDownWritePolicies` runs after **green** runs too (previously only the two red modes tore down, so every successful run left its policies resident); the **race** half is untouched and is what this entry is for. Two further consequences worth carrying: a mutation that "survives" because another leg's policies were resident **reads as a survival**, so mutation-testing evidence taken under concurrency is only as good as its mechanism proof; and `tearDownRowVisRLS` drops a hard-coded list of policies, so it silently goes stale every time a card adds one. · _run `20260802`, found by card B1's G6 fix round while diagnosing a Go failure in a card with no Go in its diff; number reserved by the orchestrator_ · new (**destination: NEXT milestone (run mechanics / test isolation)** — it does not redden a named key result on the current OKR page, so it routes forward under this slate's scope freeze. Leads, in ascending cost: (i) give the substrate a per-leg schema or database and an `HQ_SYNC_SUBSTRATE_DB`-style override, mirroring what `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB` does for the HQ side; (ii) derive the teardown list from the SQL files rather than a hard-coded list, so it cannot go stale; (iii) serialise substrate-touching legs at dispatch, which is the control loop's lever and costs wall-clock rather than code. 🛑 **Until one of these lands, a slate that puts two substrate-touching cards on concurrent tracks is buying a class of unattributable red** — which is exactly what `slate-20260802` §5 warned would surface if gate evidence looked contended.)
B-52 [**destination: NEXT milestone**] The import-reachability guard is blind to any script reference that is not a literal — `build-sw.js`'s `checkImportReachability` parses literal `src="…"` attributes and literal ES module specifiers. A page that builds a URL at runtime (`s.src = base + name`), or a module that `import()`s a computed specifier, is invisible to it, and so is a `<link rel="stylesheet">`, an `<img src>` and a CSS `@import` — the guard is deliberately scoped to the **module and script graph**, which is where absence breaks the tool rather than the favicon. **Nothing in the tree does any of this today** (measured: 30 local references across 18 precached files, all literal, all script-or-module), which is exactly why it is filed rather than built — a resolver for computed URLs is a static-analysis project, not a build hook, and it would be guarding a case that does not exist. The honest statement of the guard's reach is "every literal script edge", not "every edge", and this entry exists so the next person to widen it knows which half is missing. · _run `overnight-20260802`, card P1 `build-deploy-manifest-integrity`, found while bounding the parser's scope_ (new)
