B-55 [**destination: NEXT milestone, with B-52** — same file, same function, one sitting. Lead: do not reach for an AST parser to fix a nuisance. The cheap correct step is to strip `//` line comments and `/* */` blocks before matching (a tokenizer-lite pass, ~10 lines), which kills the comment case outright; the string-literal case is rarer and can stay, because a false positive that fails loudly against a machine-generated file is still better than a silent miss. If it does bite `vendor/rxdb.bundle.js` before then, the one-line unblock is a `SKIP_PARSE` allow-list keyed on the URL — but that trades this nuisance for a genuine blind spot on the single largest precached asset, so it is a last resort and must be filed when used.] The reachability parser fires on NON-edges — a specifier inside a comment or a string literal is read as a reference — measured against `build-sw.js`'s exported `collectLocalRefs`: `'// import x from "./commented.js";'` yields the ref `./commented.js`, and a specifier embedded in a string literal yields one too. The regexes are line-oriented and have no notion of lexical context. **It fails CLOSED** — a phantom ref that resolves outside the precache throws, so no `sw.js` is written and the build is loud — which makes it a nuisance rather than an outage, and is why it is filed rather than fixed. 🛑 **The realistic trigger is not hand-written code.** `vendor/rxdb.bundle.js` is precached, 495 KiB, and **machine-generated** by `vendor/build-vendor.sh`; a future regeneration whose output happens to emit a relative specifier inside a comment banner or a string breaks the build with a message pointing at a file nobody edited and a "reference" that does not exist. 🛑 **This is the false-POSITIVE half. Already-filed B-52 records only the false-NEGATIVE half** (computed `.src`, computed `import()`), and the two want opposite fixes — B-52 wants the parser to see more, this wants it to see less. Anyone widening one should read the other first. · _run `over