B-72 [**destination: next milestone, and it needs the operator's answer to §8 Q3 of `docs/contracts/inventory-menu-cogs.md` before it can be planned**] `/menu-cogs` omits menu items that sold but have no recipe, so the endpoint cannot name what it failed to cost — `MenuCogsHandler`'s summary query inner-joins `menu_items` to the recipe allocation set (`JOIN alloc a ON a.menu_item_id = mi.id`) and LEFT-joins sales, so row membership is decided by **having a recipe**, not by having sales. A menu item that sold 30 units with no recipe row attached is absent from `menu_items` entirely. Its ingredient spend still lands in `unallocated_cogs` — with nothing naming the menu item — so the operator sees a residual dollar figure and no way to learn which seller is uncosted. The Phase 999.2 contract published the opposite behaviour as **State C** and gave sales-processor an acceptance test for it (**Scenario 2**) that could never have passed; both were withdrawn on 2026-08-03. There is no HQ test for the case — the nearest, `TestMenuCogs_UnitsSoldZero_PerUnitIsNull`, exercises the reverse (recipe, no sales). 🛑 **Not fixed on this card by design:** `/menu-cogs` is consumed by an external system, so changing row membership adds rows to every response and is a contract change, not a bug fix. · _run `20260803`, card P6 `period-summary-contract-notice`_ (new)
B-73 [**destination: next milestone, gated on the operator's answer to §8 Q4 of `docs/contracts/inventory-menu-cogs.md`** — it is a genuine product fork (re-align the two endpoints, or formally declare them non-reconcilable and retire the check)] `/period-summary` and `/menu-cogs` stopped reconciling on 2026-06-06 and the broken invariant is still published, unmonitored — `docs/contracts/inventory-menu-cogs.md` published `SUM(menu_items[].ingredient_cost_total) + unallocated_cogs ≈ period-summary cogs_incl_tax`, within $0.10. It held when both endpoints were written and broke when the **sibling** changed: `a726029` (2026-06-05) gave `/period-summary` a `mercury_category = ANY(HQ_COGS_CATEGORY_ALLOWLIST)` filter that `/menu-cogs` does not apply, and `d41faef` (2026-06-06) folded unconfirmed `pending_purchases` into `/period-summary`'s COGS at full `ABS(bank_total)`, which `/menu-cogs` cannot see because pending rows have no `purchase_line_items` yet. **The divergence runs in both directions and is bounded by nothing** — its size depends on how much of a period is miscategorised and how much sits in the review queue, neither knowable from either response. Any sales-processor reconciliation check written against the published invariant fires spuriously. The invariant is now marked broken in the contract, but nothing on either side detects the divergence. · _run `20260803`, card P6 `period-summary-contract-notice`_ (new)
B-74 [**destination: next milestone** (operator question §8 Q3 of the period-summary contract asks whether it should be versioned at all; the answer decides the shape)] `HQ_COGS_CATEGORY_ALLOWLIST` silently restates every historical period, with no migration and no signal to the consumer — `envOrDefault("HQ_COGS_CATEGORY_ALLOWLIST", "COGS")` (`backend/cmd/server/main.go`, from `a726029`) decides which Mercury `categoryData.name` values roll up into COGS. It gates four `/period-summary` response fields — `cogs_excl_tax`, `cogs_incl_tax`, `purchase_event_count` and `completeness.pending_review_ids` — and until 2026-08-03 it appeared in **neither** contract document, so sales-processor has been consuming a figure whose scope it was never told about. Two properties make it worse than an undocumented default: **(a)** editing it changes what every *past* period reports, retroactively, with no migration and no version marker, so two runs of the same week either side of an edit disagree with no artifact explaining why; **(b)** a miscategorised transaction is invisible to every COGS figure while remaining visible in `tracked_bank_tx_ids`, so the data to detect the miss exists but nothing correlates it. Now documented in §2 of `docs/contracts/inventory-period-summary.md`; still unversioned. · _run `20260803`, card P6 `period-summary-contract-notice`_ (new)
B-75 [**destination: next milestone** — a two-line documentary fix, no code change, no KR reddened] Assumption A6 of the menu-cogs contract still publishes a false claim about where HQ rounds, and the audit's SUPERSEDED verdict hid it — `docs/contracts/inventory-menu-cogs.md` A6 reads *"HQ rounds at the Go-decode boundary (not in SQL)"*, and the sibling's `:122` invariant note repeated the same framing (*"Phase 21 rounds at the SQL level, this endpoint rounds at the Go-decode level"*). **Both endpoints round in SQL.** `MenuCogsHandler`'s summary query wraps every money expression in `ROUND(…::numeric, 2)` and the per-unit in `ROUND(…::numeric, 4)`; `loadBreakdown` and the unallocated query do the same; `PeriodSummaryHandler` likewise. Go does no rounding on either path — it scans an already-rounded numeric into a `float64`. The 2026-08-03 audit marked A6 **SUPERSEDED** (its $0.10 tolerance is moot now that the invariant is broken outright) and so never checked the rounding-locus half, which is a separate and still-live false code claim. Low blast radius — nobody's decoder changes — but it is the kind of unexamined sentence that survives inside a row that *looks* audited. · _run `20260803`, card P6 `period-summary-contract-notice`, found by the G6 fix round; **out of scope under the card's scope freeze, which was F1–F10 only**_ (new)
B-76 [**destination: NEXT milestone (test isolation / gate integrity), and it belongs with B-50 and B-35 as one sitting** — all three are "the harness cannot tell whose state it is looking at"] The full-suite Playwright gate runs against a database that carries state between runs — a red can be an artifact of a previous run, and so can a green — run `20260803`'s final gate returned one failure, `tests/onboarding.spec.js:689 › Manager tab › sign-off form requires readiness rating (notes optional)`, a 30s timeout waiting for `[data-action="view-training"][data-template-id=…]`. **The title is on no armed-reds list**, so it was investigated rather than absorbed. Measured, in order: tonight's run touches **zero** onboarding files (`git diff --name-only 60b9edb HEAD` matches nothing under `onboarding`); re-run in isolation on the merged tree against the shared `hq_test_e2e` → **FAILED** (so not the B-45 load-sensitivity class, which passes in isolation); the same test on the **base commit `60b9edb`** against the shared DB → **FAILED** (so not introduced by this run); base commit with a **fresh** DB → **PASSED**; merged tree with a **fresh** DB → **PASSED**. 🛑 **The behaviour is symmetric across the base and merged trees — dirty fails on both, fresh passes on both** — which is what rules out the run as the cause and identifies accumulated state as the mechanism. `playwright.config.js:25` defaults every run to the single database `hq_test_e2e` and `TEST_DB_NAME` is opt-in, so consecutive suite runs share one accumulating dataset; the config's own comment already argues for separate databases *between the Go and Playwright suites* and stops one step short of arguing for a fresh one *per run*. **The half that should worry us is not the false red — it is the false green.** A suite whose fixtures accumulate can pass because a prior run left a row behind, and every full-suite figure in this milestone — including tonight's 778 — was taken under that condition. This is the same defect class as B-16 (a dropped test database reading as a passing suite) approached from the opposite side. · _run `20260803`, final gate, found by the orchestrator when a non-baseline title failed and the four-way base/merged × dirty/fresh matrix was run to attribute it_ (new)
B-77 [**destination: the night-crew CLONE, next milestone — this is a tooling change, not an hq one** (same routing as **B-60**)] `run-evidence check` cannot see a scaffolded repo's closeout or conflict log — so every hq slate night reads `no-run-evidence`, and the next `/nc-run` is told the night never happened — measured at closeout of run `20260803`: with `reference/closeout-20260803.md` **and** `reference/conflicts-20260803.md` both written and committed under `.night-crew/knowledge/reference/`, `night-crew run-evidence check --repo . --run 20260803` returns **`no-run-evidence` ("nothing says this run ever executed")**. Cause, read at source in the night-crew clone (`internal/runevidence/runevidence.go`): line **173** resolves the committed scorecard correctly as `.night-crew/knowledge/scorecard/<runid>.jsonl`, but lines **177**, **218** and **221** hardcode `filepath.Join(root, "reference", …)` for the two artifacts a slate run actually produces — the **closeout record** and the **conflict log**. hq has **no root `reference/`** (the `launch-prompt` verb reports it "directory not present" and resolves to the scaffolded knowledge home), so both slate-layer sources are looked for in a directory that does not exist and the oracle correctly concludes it found nothing. 🛑 **This is the B-130 defect class exactly, one verb later.** `launch-prompt` was taught to search *both* homes precisely because a scaffolded target repo keeps its reference material under `.night-crew/knowledge/reference/`; `run-evidence` was not given the same treatment, and the two verbs now disagree about where this repo's artifacts live. **The consequence is a launch-time hazard, not a reporting nit:** `/nc-run` §1 checks run-evidence *before* cutting a branch, and on the next launch it will report that slate `20260803` never ran — while §2's guard, once this branch is merged at triage, reads clean and says "proceed." That is the exact combination the §1 check was added to prevent, and it would re-execute an archived slate. It also means **every hq slate run from `20260721` onward has been invisible to the oracle** for the same reason, which is consistent with the skill's own note that those runs read `ran-did-not-close` before the closeout record existed — the record now exists and is still unread. · _run `20260803`, closeout; found by the orchestrator running the verb to confirm its own closeout record was legible, and reading `runevidence.go` when it was not_ (new)
B-78 [**destination: next milestone, and it rides any card that touches `night-crew.toml`** — a two-line documentary fix, no behaviour change] `night-crew.toml`'s footprint comment carries a false "re-verified at landing" claim — `sync` selects six spec files, not the four it names — measured at morning triage of run `20260803` by an adversarial subagent: `night-crew.toml:59-62` asserts *"As a Playwright path regex these four tokens select exactly workflows / persistence / sync / repro-cut-task .spec.js and nothing else — no other spec filename in tests/ contains any of them (re-verified at landing)."* At HEAD the token `sync` matches **six** specs: `states-sync-rxdb-conflict-notice`, `sync-rxdb-client`, `sync-rxdb-conflict-notice`, `sync-rxdb-conflict`, `sync-schema`, `sync`. The **direction is safe** — over-inclusion means more tests run, never fewer — so no gate has been weakened and no regression escaped through it. The defect is that a **false factual claim sits inside a gate-configuration file, carrying an explicit "re-verified" stamp that invites trust**, and CLAUDE.md names a narrow footprint entry as exactly how a regression escapes a green gate. A future editor reading the comment as true could "correct" the tag back toward the four files it names and land in under-inclusion, which is the unsafe direction. · _morning triage 2026-08-02, run `20260803`; found by the adversarial verifier measuring the claim rather than reading it_ (new)
B-79 [**destination: the successor cutover card** — it is already editing this file and this scope model] S1a's unknown-`scope.mode` refusal is named "must survive" in its own merge-intent and is pinned by no test — replacing it with `if (false)` leaves the suite fully green — measured at morning triage of run `20260803` by mutation: `sync-rxdb/client.js:654-660` refuses an unrecognised `scope.mode`, and `merge-intents/merge-intent-s1a-sync-cutover-list-scope.md` lists that refusal as **must-survive item 1**. Neutering the guard leaves `tests/sync-rxdb-client.spec.js` at **55/55 passing**; no test anywhere passes a `mode` value other than `'list'`. **Severity is low and should not be overstated** — the fill branch's `checklistId`/`templateId` refusals still catch a malformed scope, so a neutered guard cannot widen into a pull-whole, which is the failure that would actually matter (and which `architecture/C-2` exists to prevent). What makes it worth filing is the *class*: a merge-intent's "what must survive" list is the artifact a 3am merger resolves conflicts against, so an entry on that list which no test defends is a claim the next merger will trust and the suite will not enforce. Seven of S1a's other guards were mutation-tested green-to-red at triage; this is the one that was not. · _morning triage 2026-08-02, run `20260803`; found by the adversarial verifier mutating each must-survive claim in turn_ (new)
B-80 [**destination: the LAUNCH-PROMPT isolation stanza, and it is a same-milestone fix — the hazard is live tonight, not next milestone**] Every Playwright invocation now DROPs the shared default `hq_test_e2e`, and cards are not issued a `TEST_DB_NAME` — so two concurrent legs no longer coexist, the later one destroys the earlier one mid-suite — B-76's fix put `DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS <name>; CREATE DATABASE <name>;` (`scripts/reset-e2e-db.js:111-131`) at the head of `webServer.command` (`playwright.config.js:109`), where it fires on **every** `npx playwright test` invocation, full or subset. `TEST_DB_NAME` defaults to the single shared `hq_test_e2e` (`scripts/reset-e2e-db.js:77`). The run's per-card isolation issues a unique `TEST_PORT` and a unique `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB` but **not** a unique `TEST_DB_NAME` (`launch-20260803.md:97-98` names `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB`, `TEST_PORT` and a scratchpad directory and nothing else; 🛑 **and tonight's own `launch-20260804.md` carries no per-card isolation stanza at all** — grep it for `unique`, `TEST_PORT`, `TEST_DB_NAME`, `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB` and every one is absent, as it is in `slate-20260804.md`. The `slate-20260803.md:444` stanza that did exist says "a unique database prefix", which reads as covering this and demonstrably did not: card A1's own implementer ran `TEST_PORT=8201` against the bare `hq_test_e2e`). **Two legs differing only in `TEST_PORT` therefore share one database, and the reset makes that fatal rather than merely dirty** — before B-76 they merely polluted each other; now the second leg's first link drops the first leg's database out from under a server mid-suite. G6 demonstrated it by execution: an in-flight `onboarding` subset on `:8211` collapsed to **3 failed / 27 did not run / 25 passed / exit 1** when a second leg's first link ran. 🛑 **B-76's resolution note in this same file justified declining the per-run-database alternative on the grounds that "the collision half is already carried by `TEST_DB_NAME` + `TEST_PORT` (every card is issued its own pair)" — that premise is false and has been struck in place in the B-76 entry as part of this filing** (see the CORRECTED 2026-08-04 sentence there). A resolved entry justifying itself with a false premise is how the next reader gets misled. · _G6 adversarial review of card A1 `e2e-gate-database-isolation`, run `overnight-20260804`, found by execution when a second leg was started alongside an in-flight one; the launch-prompt half re-measured in the A1 fix round_ (new)
B-81 [**resolved 2026-08-04**] The reset banner went to stdout, which Playwright's `webServer` discards by default — so a gate log carried zero evidence the reset had run — `scripts/reset-e2e-db.js`'s CLI branch announced its reset with `console.log`. It runs as the first link of `playwright.config.js`'s `webServer.command`, and the webServer plugin pipes the child's stdout **only** when `webServer.stdout === 'pipe'` (`node_modules/playwright/lib/plugins/webServerPlugin.js:126`), an option documented as defaulting to `'ignore'` (`node_modules/playwright/types/test.d.ts:10285-10289`). stderr **is** piped by default (same file, `:10281-10283`) — which is exactly why the Go server's slog lines appear in every gate log and the banner did not. On a successful DROP psql emits no NOTICE either, so **a gate log contained no evidence whatsoever that the database had been reset** — the precise property the line's own comment claims to deliver: *"a silent reset is indistinguishable from no reset at all, which is B-76's mechanism."* B-76's fix was real but unobservable from the artifact triage actually reads. · _G6 adversarial review of card A1, run `overnight-20260804`; confirmed by execution across five runs — the banner never appeared while `[WebServer]` lines did_ (**✅ RESOLVED 2026-08-04 — A1 fix round, commit `7addc92`, branch `card/a1-e2e-gate-database-isolation`.** One line: `console.log` → `console.error`. Chosen over adding `stdout: 'pipe'` to the `webServer` block for blast radius — the config switch would also un-silence the Go server's entire stdout stream on every leg in the repo, to fix one line that already lives in the file that owns the banner. Banner content is unchanged (it names the database and the host:port; that content is the evidence). **Verified by execution, not by inspection**, with an A/B control inside a *single* invocation — a temporary `console.log` control line carried alongside the `console.error` banner: `TEST_PORT=8221 TEST_DB_NAME=hq_test_e2e_a1fix npx playwright test "db-isolation" --retries=0` emitted `[WebServer] ── reset hq_test_e2e_a1fix on localhost:5433 ──` as **line 1** of Playwright's output while `grep -c CONTROL-STDOUT` over the same log returned **0**. Same process, same run: stderr reaches the log, stdout does not. Control line reverted; only the `console.error` change ships. Suite green (`1 passed`); G4 `node build-sw.js` twice → 31 precached both times, tree clean.)
B-82 [**destination: next milestone, with B-09/B-86 — all three are "a green gate that did not run the thing it claims to grade"**] `tests/db-isolation.spec.js` never runs on a seam-confined subset leg — the test that guards the reset is blind to the reset's removal on exactly the cards most likely to remove it — the subset positional regex is built by unioning the `[e2e.seams]` tags of the seams a card touched (`night-crew.toml:36-60`, expanded into `subset = 'npx playwright test "{tags}"'` at `:34`). The full tag vocabulary is `inventory`, `recipes`, `workflows`, `persistence`, `sync`, `repro-cut-task`, `onboarding`, `users`, `purchasing` (🛑 the G6 report listed only the first six; the other three exist — the conclusion is unchanged because **none of the nine matches `db-isolation`** as a path regex). So on any confined card the reset still fires structurally — it is in `webServer.command`, which no CLI argument can skip — but **the test that would catch its removal does not run**. A future card that "tidies" the reset out of `webServer.command` while touching only `backend/internal/inventory` would go green. Note the escape is narrow by luck, not design: `scripts/reset-e2e-db.js` and `playwright.config.js` are unmapped, so a card editing *them* de-confines to the full suite. The exposure is a card that removes the reset **as collateral** while confined elsewhere. **Secondary vacuous-pass path:** `--project=bdd` alone (`task bdd`) never runs the spec, so it leaves no marker behind — and the following run then passes trivially, since the assertion is "no marker present" and the marker's absence is indistinguishable from a successful reset. The test is cross-process by construction and a skipped invocation silently re-arms it. · _G6 adversarial review of card A1, run `overnight-20260804`; tag vocabulary re-measured against `night-crew.toml` in the A1 fix round_ (new)
B-83 [**destination: next milestone, with B-16/B-35 — same sitting**] The reset guard is name-only — it checks the database name and never the host, port or credentials, so it would happily DROP a correctly-named database on a production server — `assertTestDatabaseName` (`scripts/reset-e2e-db.js:93-104`) tests `TEST_DB_NAME` against `/^hq_test(?:_[a-z0-9]+)*$/` and nothing else. `resolveE2eDb` (`:71-91`) reads `DB_HOST`, `DB_PORT`, `DB_USER` and `DB_PASS` straight from the environment with no assertion of any kind. Therefore `DB_HOST=<prod-host> TEST_DB_NAME=hq_test node scripts/reset-e2e-db.js` drops `hq_test` **on a remote server** without complaint — the guard sees a well-formed test name and waves it through. This is **within the card's stated scope** as written ("refuse any database whose *name* is not a test database") and the card delivered that faithfully; the filing is that the scope is narrower than the property the surrounding prose implies — B-16/B-35's lesson is "the harness cannot eat the wrong database", and a name check alone does not establish that. Severity is low today (no prod Postgres is reachable from this box under these coordinates) and would rise the moment one is. · _G6 adversarial review of card A1, run `overnight-20260804`_ (new)
B-84 [**destination: next milestone, and it rides whatever card first makes `NIGHTCREW_ENV_URL` the real gate path — it is not actionable before then**] With `NIGHTCREW_ENV_URL` set the reset does not run and the test that would say so `test.skip()`s — the one shape in which B-76's fix is inert AND reports nothing — `playwright.config.js:65` makes `webServer` `undefined` when `NIGHTCREW_ENV_URL` is set, so `webServer.command` — and with it the entire reset — never executes. `tests/db-isolation.spec.js:48-51` then `test.skip()`s rather than failing. **Both halves are correct behaviour**, and this entry is not a request to change them: in that mode a provisioned ephemeral stack owns its own database, the localhost coordinates name a *different* database, and asserting against it would grade the wrong one — a worse outcome than not grading it. It is also demonstrably **not** the current gate path (`night-crew.toml`'s `[e2e]` legs run `npx playwright test` directly and A1's evidence was taken through `webServer`). What is worth filing is the shape: this is the single configuration in which the card's deliverable is **fully inert and silent** — no reset, no banner (B-81's fix included, since the banner is printed by the code that does not run), and no test noticing. If the run mode ever flips to provisioned envs, B-76 quietly stops holding and nothing in the suite says so. · _G6 adversarial review of card A1, run `overnight-20260804`_ (new)
B-85 [**destination: next milestone; trivial, and it rides any card touching `Taskfile.yml`**] `task bdd` points at `DB_PORT: '5432'`, which black-holes SYN on this box — pre-existing, but B-76's reset changed the failure's SHAPE from a fast connect error to a 60s stall — `Taskfile.yml:82-86` sets `DB_HOST: localhost` / `DB_PORT: '5432'` for the `bdd` task. Host `:5432` is bound by `infra-postgres-1` (slack-trading), which has no `yumyums` role — every other site in the repo uses **5433** (`yumyums-dev-pg`), including `test:go` at `:92` and `test` at `:108`. **Broken before this card and broken after it** — the card neither caused nor fixed it, and no gate leg runs `task bdd`. The reason to file it is that the *symptom* moved: previously it failed when the Go server tried to connect; now `node scripts/reset-e2e-db.js` is the first link of `webServer.command`, so the failure lands on the psql reset instead and presents as a **hang until `webServer.timeout: 60000` expires** (`playwright.config.js:116`) rather than as a named connection error. Worth knowing before someone spends the 60s stall looking for it in the wrong place. Note this is the same 5432/5433 inconsistency the card *did* remove from `test:ui`'s deleted inline psql block — `bdd` is the copy that survived. · _G6 adversarial review of card A1, run `overnight-20260804`_ (new)
B-86 [**destination: next milestone, with B-09 and B-82 — one sitting on "a green gate that did not run what it claims to grade"**] `verify-test-harness.sh`'s A2 floor is stale at 20 while the repo resolves 27 — a silent drop of up to seven spec files still passes the check built to catch exactly that — `scripts/verify-test-harness.sh:78` sets `MIN_SPEC_FILES="${H1_MIN_SPEC_FILES:-20}"`, and its own comment at `:74-75` says the floor is *"20 (19 static under ./tests + 1 generated under .features-gen/). It ratchets UP as spec files are added; it must never ratchet down silently."* Measured at HEAD: `ls tests/*.spec.js` returns **26**, plus the one generated BDD spec — **27 resolved**. Nobody ratcheted the floor as the seven specs were added, so A2 passes with as few as 20 files reaching the runner. **That gap IS the B-09 failure the check exists to catch**: a suite that silently resolves fewer spec files than the repo contains reports success for tests it never ran. The check is not broken — it is doing exactly what it says with a number that stopped being true, which is the more dangerous state because it reports PASS. 🛑 Note the check is also **self-invalidating on the wrong side**: because the floor is a `-ge` comparison, adding spec files never makes it fail, so nothing will ever force the ratchet. It has to be maintained deliberately or replaced. · _G6 adversarial review of card A1, run `overnight-20260804`; spec count re-measured at HEAD in the A1 fix round_ (new)
B-87 [**destination: next milestone, with B-82 and B-86 — all three are "the confined gate does not run the set it claims to"**, though note this one errs toward more coverage, not less] A Playwright CLI path filter matches the ABSOLUTE path, so a worktree directory name containing a seam token silently turns a confined subset into the FULL suite — the gate over-runs and its "which tests ran" claim is wrong — `night-crew.toml:34`'s `subset = 'npx playwright test "{tags}"'` passes the `[e2e.seams]` tags as bare positional args. Those become unanchored regexes tested against the **absolute** file path: `node_modules/playwright/lib/util.js:183` `forceRegExp` → `new RegExp(pattern, "gi")` (no anchors, matches anywhere in the string); `util.js:128` `createFileMatcher` → `re.test(filePath)`; `runner/projectUtils.js:147-161` `collectFilesForProject` collects from the **resolved absolute** `testDir`, and `runner/loadUtils.js:63-71` feeds those absolute paths to the filter. So a **directory component** of the checkout path can satisfy a token meant to name a spec file. Measured from `hq-worktrees/a2-workflows-autosavefield-phantom` with the `workflows.html` seam subset `["workflows","persistence","sync","repro-cut-task"]`: `--list --project=chromium workflows` selects **786 tests / 26 files — the entire chromium suite** — because the directory component `a2-workflows-…` matches the `workflows` regex on *every* file in the tree; the same invocation from `/home/jcole/projects/hq` selects **86 / 1**. 🛑 **The consequence first proposed for this finding was WRONG, and is recorded as wrong so it does not become folklore.** The candidate filed on `card/a2-workflows-autosavefield-phantom` claimed that a worktree named for a *different* app — say `b-inventory-…` — "would make `[e2e] subset` select that app's specs **INSTEAD of** the seam's and report a green subset that never ran the seam it was confined to." **That does not follow, and G6 refuted it at source.** CLI path filters are **OR'd**, not intersected: `createFileMatcherFromArguments` (`util.js:124-127`) folds **all** positional args into **one** matcher whose closure (`:141-160`) returns `true` on the first regex that hits; `runner/tasks.js:243` pushes that single matcher onto `config.loadFileFilters`, so `loadUtils.js:68`'s `loadFileFilters.every(…)` iterates a **one-element** array. A directory-component match can therefore only ever produce a **superset** — it can never exclude a spec that would otherwise match. A worktree named `b-inventory-…` running tokens `workflows persistence sync` selects exactly the intended files, because `inventory` is not one of the tokens. **The under-coverage failure mode is unreachable by this mechanism.** Confirmed empirically three ways: `--list --project=chromium workflows inventory` from `/home/jcole/projects/hq` → **238** = 86 + 152 (`inventory` alone = 152), which is the OR; and from the `a2-workflows-…` worktree, `persistence` **alone** → **32 / 1 file**, i.e. a token the directory name does *not* contain is not widened at all, while `workflows` alone → 786 / 26 = the same count as passing no filter whatsoever. **The real consequence:** a worktree directory name containing a filter token silently turns a confined subset into the **full suite**. The gate **over-runs** — its runtime, its isolation assumptions, and its "which tests ran" claim are all not what they say. A card budgeted for a 3-minute confined subset silently spends 21 minutes; a leg that believed it was touching only the `workflows` seam's fixtures touches every seam's; and the merge-intent line "gate ran the confined subset" is false while the gate is green. This is a sibling of **B-76** in kind — *the harness cannot tell you what it actually ran* — but it is **not** a silent-skip risk, and the confined-gate-misses-a-regression direction is unreachable this way. · _run `overnight-20260804`, card A2 `workflows-autosavefield-phantom`, found by the implementer when the confined gate returned 787 tests instead of a subset; the proposed consequence refuted by G6 at source and re-measured by execution in the A2 fix round_ (new)
B-88 [**destination: next milestone, with B-79 — both are "a named invariant is pinned by nothing that would notice its removal"**] Rule 2's guard is bypassable and already bypassed — `workflows.html` reads RxDB through `window.HQSync.db`, which the three-symbol assertion does not watch, on an eagerly-mounted page-load path that runs offline — `tests/sync-rxdb-client.spec.js:1468-1470` is three `expect(src).not.toContain(…)` assertions over the **source text** of `workflows.html`, naming exactly `HQSync.createDatabase`, `HQSync.startReplication` and `HQSync.client`. It is cited — in `designs/offline-ownership.md` before the A4 fix round, and in the C1/C2 card charters — as the enforcement of the rule *"nothing may read from RxDB on a code path that can execute offline."* Measured: `workflows.html:3588-3592` is `function defaultStore(){ const db = window.HQSync && window.HQSync.db; if (db && db.conflict_records) return createRxdbConflictStore(db.conflict_records); return createMemoryConflictStore([]); }`, mounted **eagerly at page load** (`workflows.html:3639`, `window.HQConflictNotice.instance = window.HQConflictNotice.mount()`), on a path that runs offline. **`window.HQSync.db` is a fourth route the guard does not name**, so the assertion is green today and would stay green. 🛑 **The rule is therefore gated by the database not existing, not by the test.** `defaultStore()` falls through to the empty in-memory store only because nothing calls `createHQSyncDatabase`, which is what leaves `window.HQSync.db` undefined — `sync-rxdb/bootstrap.js:82-83` exposes it as a deferred handle and no production page calls it. **Harmless today, and only today: the first card that creates a database — `sync-hard-cutover` is the obvious one — breaks the rule with no diff to anything the guard watches, and no gate reds.** The failure class is the one this repo keeps arming against and B-70/B-79 are siblings of: **a green assertion that does not cover the thing it is cited as covering.** · _G6 adversarial review of card A4 `offline-ownership-design-note`, run `overnight-20260804`; the read path re-verified at source in the A4 fix round. The design note's enforcement claim is struck in place and corrected there; the code-side gap is this entry._ (promoted → skeleton-offline-ownership-honesty (roadmap round 2026-08-05))
B-89 [**destination: next milestone, with B-65 — one sitting on "the test plants a shape production does not write"**] `cachedGrantSlugs()` returns `[]` unconditionally on every real client — `index.html` writes `hq_apps` as an identity envelope `{uid, apps}` and `bootstrap.js` still `Array.isArray`-gates on the bare array that shape replaced — 🛑 **a live code defect, found while re-verifying class row 5 for the A4 design note; out of that card's docs-only contract and NOT fixed there.** `index.html:232-235`'s `writeCachedApps` does `localStorage.setItem(APPS_CACHE_KEY, JSON.stringify({uid:deviceId, apps:apps}))`, and `readCachedApps` (`:221-231`) **deliberately discards** a bare array at `:228` (`if(!env||Array.isArray(env)||env.uid!==deviceId||…)return null`) as pre-card legacy whose owner is unknown — fail closed, obligation 7(a). That is correct and intended. But `sync-rxdb/bootstrap.js:62-71` was never moved to the envelope: it does `const apps = JSON.parse(raw); return Array.isArray(apps) ? apps.map((a) => a && a.slug).filter(Boolean) : [];` — **the envelope is an object, not an array, so the ternary takes the `[]` branch every time.** The cached grant list that decides **what to replicate** (obligation 4 / decision 56, the narrow claim + umbrella expansion at `expandGrantSlugs`) is therefore **always empty on a real client**, and there is no error, no warning and no visible symptom — the `catch` at `:68-70` is not even reached. 🛑 **No test catches it because the test plants the shape the app no longer writes:** `tests/sync-rxdb-client.spec.js:1385` does `localStorage.setItem('hq_apps', JSON.stringify([{slug:'inventory'},{slug:'operations'}]))` — a **bare array** — in an `addInitScript`, so the expansion assertions downstream pass on a fixture `index.html` would itself discard. **Sibling in kind to B-65**: a test that is green on a shape the application never produces, guarding a path that is dead in production. Latent in consequence *today* only because replication is never started; it arms with the same deploy that arms §4's reconciliation gap. · _run `overnight-20260804`, card A4 `offline-ownership-design-note`, found by the fix-round implementer while opening the row-5 citations; both shapes read at source_ (promoted → shipped-bug-sweep (roadmap round 2026-08-05))
B-90 [**destination: next milestone, with B-59 — same comment block, fix in one sitting**] `build-sw.js`'s own safety-argument comment says the precache is "still 29 files" when it is 31 — a reader checking the number against reality gets a false negative on the argument — `build-sw.js:457` reads *"the precache is still 29 files"*, inside the `:455-458` comment block explaining why deleting the `api-cache` `cacheKeyWillBeUsed` hook is invisible to every gate (*"build-sw.js still exits 0, the precache is still 29 files, the SW still installs. Only [B1-XT-01] and [B1-XT-02] fail."*). Measured: `node build-sw.js` prints `SW built: 31 files precached`, and `CLAUDE.md` states the count is 31. The count moved when `vendor/rxdb.bundle.js`, the five `sync-rxdb/*.js` modules and `sync-schema/collections.js` were added to `globPatterns`; the comment did not. 🛑 **The damage is specific to where the number sits.** This is not a stale number in prose — it is a **load-bearing premise in the repo's own argument about what a gate would and would not notice.** A reader auditing that argument runs the build, sees 31 against a claimed 29, and has to decide whether the comment is stale or the invariant is broken; the honest reading of a mismatch is "something moved and nobody looked," which is exactly the alarm B-37's reachability guard exists to raise. A false alarm in the one comment that teaches people to trust that number is worse than no comment. **Same comment block as B-59**, which flags a different defect in the same four lines (it names two tests where only one actually fails) — the block should be fixed once, for both. · _G6 adversarial review of card A4 `offline-ownership-design-note`, run `overnight-20260804`; count re-measured by running `node build-sw.js` twice in the A4 fix round_ (new)
B-91 [**destination: next milestone — a small, contained docs+comment correction; can ride any card already opening `sync.js` or `workflows.html`**] Three sites in the tree still say `hq_offline_v1` holds ONE store — it has held two since the database went to version 2, and the citations they carry have all moved — `designs/offline-save-honesty.md:13-14` states *"`submitOp` (`sync.js:695`) has no offline queue — IndexedDB `hq_offline_v1` holds one store, `submitQueue` (`sync.js:51`), and `drainQueue` (`sync.js:560`) drains only that"*; the **same file** repeats the singular a third time at `:142` (*"`submitQueue` in IndexedDB (`sync.js:51`) + `drainQueue`"*, in the today-vs-after-cutover table); and `workflows.html:384-386` repeats *"the IndexedDB queue that today holds submissions only (`sync.js:51`)"*. Measured: `sync.js:48` opens the database at **version 2** and `onupgradeneeded` creates **two** object stores — `submitQueue` at `sync.js:51-53` and `syncMeta` at `sync.js:54-56` (the Lamport clock's persisted state, `LamportClock` `sync.js:108-139`). The function citations have also drifted: `submitOp` is now `sync.js:781-797` and `drainQueue` is now `sync.js:632`. **The mechanism each site describes is unchanged and still correct** — an unsent field answer genuinely has no store — so this is not a wrong conclusion; it is a wrong inventory underneath a right conclusion. 🛑 **This is precisely the collapse card A4's non-negotiable #2 exists to prevent, already in the tree before A4 was written.** `submitQueue` and `syncMeta` have **different fates**: retire the op-log and `syncMeta` becomes dead weight while `submitQueue` survives untouched, so a sentence reading "`hq_offline_v1` holds one store" conceals a whole class from anyone who opens these files looking for where data went. `designs/offline-ownership.md` §1b names all three sites and publishes the correct split; these three sites still contradict it. · _run `overnight-20260804`, card A4 `offline-ownership-design-note`; two sites named in the deliverable at authoring time, the third (`:142`) found by G6 and the line numbers re-verified in the A4 fix round_ (new)
B-92 [**destination: next milestone — gate hygiene, one line in the `webServer.command` chain**] The in-worktree `npx playwright test` path never generates `version.json`, so that stack serves a static tree missing a file `sw.js` precaches and `index.html` now displays — 🛑 **headline narrowed 2026-08-04 (A6 fix round, G6 finding 2).** It previously read *"The Playwright gate path never generates `version.json`"*, which is broader than the body below supports and broader than the code: `night-crew.toml:12-16` declares an **ephemeral** env built from `docker-compose.nc.yml` → `backend/Dockerfile:58-64`, which **does** generate the file, and in that mode `playwright.config.js:65` makes `webServer` `undefined` entirely so none of the machinery below is even reached. The gap was only ever the **in-worktree `npx playwright test`** path. A headline is what a future reader triages from, so it names the path that actually had the gap. — `version.json` is git-ignored on purpose (`.gitignore:13`): `build-sw.js`'s `writeVersionJson()` (`build-sw.js:291-297`) writes it locally from `package.json` `"version"`, and `backend/Dockerfile:57-64` regenerates it into the image from the authoritative `version.go` `Frontend` constant, because `sw.js` precaches it and a 404 aborts the whole service-worker install. `tests/sw-manifest.spec.js:15` already encodes the exception (`GENERATED_BUT_SHIPPED = ['version.json']`). **The gap is the test stack.** `task test` generates the file via its `sw` dep (`Taskfile.yml:39`), but `night-crew.toml:33-34` runs `npx playwright test` **directly** for both `suite` and `subset`, and `playwright.config.js`'s `webServer.command` serves `STATIC_DIR=../` — the bare worktree. In a worktree where `node build-sw.js` has never run, `GET /version.json` **404s**, and it was measured doing exactly that on this card: the first green run of `tests/version-badge.spec.js` had 4/5 passing with the unmocked test reporting `Expected: "v1.4.0" Received: "v—"`. 🛑 **Not a production defect** — the Dockerfile block covers prod, and the app's response is honest (`data-state="unknown"`, no API fallback). The defect is that a *missing build artifact* and a *broken version line* are indistinguishable at the gate, on the one leg where nothing regenerates the artifact. Sibling of **B-76** in kind: the night-crew gate path skips something `task test` does, and the omission presents as a test result rather than as a setup error. **Mitigated but not fixed in-card:** `tests/version-badge.spec.js`'s unmocked test now asserts `GET /version.json` is 200 first, with a failure message naming `node build-sw.js`, so the artifact gap fails loud instead of reading as a code defect. · _run `overnight-20260804`, card A6 `app-version-badge`; found by execution when the unmocked test rendered `v—` in a fresh worktree_ (new)
B-93 [**destination: next milestone, with B-81/B-82/B-86/B-87 — the "a gate cannot tell you what it actually did" cluster**] The orchestrator's own final-gate capture was piped through `tail`, destroying the "exactly one summary block" check — run `overnight-20260804`'s final G2 (Playwright) was invoked as `npx playwright test --retries=0 2>&1 | tail -30`, so the retained log holds only the last 30 lines. HQ's verification ladder specifies **"exactly one summary block; two blocks under one header = an invalidated run"** — a check that requires the *whole* log and cannot be performed on a tail. The gate was judged sound on indirect evidence instead (test indices consecutive through 792, `786 passed + 6 skipped + 0 failed = 792` reconciling exactly, exit 0), which is strong but is **not the specified check**. 🛑 This repo has already been bitten by this exact class: `card-actuals.md` records a `go build ./...` whose error was **masked by a pipe into `tail`** and printed a false green. Consequence: the one gate whose figures the whole run is reported on is the one gate that cannot evidence its own validity — the same shape as B-81 (a reset that could not be seen in a log) and B-92 (a gate that skipped a step `task test` performs). · _run `overnight-20260804`, found by the orchestrator against its own closeout_ (new)
B-77 [] DIAGNOSIS ADDED 2026-08-04 (run `overnight-20260804`) — the blindness has a concrete, two-part mechanism, established by running the oracle after a night that demonstrably ran and closed. `night-crew run-evidence check --repo . --run 20260804` returns `no-run-evidence` and prints the paths it consulted: (1) it looks for `.night-crew/runs/**<runid>**/summary.json`, `metrics.jsonl` and `journal.jsonl` — i.e. a directory literally named `20260804` — but this repo's run directories are named `**2026-08-04-autonomous**`, so none of the three can ever be found here; and (2) it looks for `**reference/**conflicts-<runid>.md` at the **repo root**, while this repo is scaffolded and its conflict log lives at `**.night-crew/knowledge/reference/**conflicts-20260804.md`. 🛑 **This is the same class as B-130** — a verb resolving a knowledge path against the development clone's layout rather than a scaffolded target repo's — which was **fixed for `launch-prompt` and never for `run-evidence`**. Consequence, and it is the one that costs a night: a completed run reads `ran-did-not-close` or `no-run-evidence` at the next launch, and `/nc-run` §1 routes it to the unfinished-night path. Verified tonight in both directions: `no-run-evidence` **before** the run (correct — nothing had happened) and `no-run-evidence` **after** a 4-of-4 night with a committed `reference/closeout-20260804.md`, four merge-intent notes and a four-entry conflict log (wrong). · lead: resolve both path families the way `launch-prompt` already does — search the scaffolded knowledge home as well as the root, and match the run directory by run-id **prefix** rather than exact name. Until then the launch prompt's standing warning is the only mitigation, and it must keep being written into every slate. ()
B-132 [promoted → shipped-bug-sweep (roadmap round 2026-08-05)] `workflows.html:708` throws an uncaught `IndexSizeError` on every completed checklist submission, orphaning a full-screen canvas over the app — the guard at `:706` tests `if(p.life<=0)continue;`, then `:707` does `p.life-=0.015`, so a particle at `life=0.01` clears the guard, becomes `-0.005`, and `ctx.arc(p.x,p.y,p.size*p.life,…)` at `:708` gets a negative radius. `:709` clamps `globalAlpha` with `Math.max(0,…)`; the radius is not clamped anywhere. Observed **28 times in one full-suite run**, and once per isolated run, on **both** `dev` and the branch: `Uncaught IndexSizeError: … The radius provided (-0.0219237) is negative. at workflows.html:708`. **Consequence:** the throw escapes the `requestAnimationFrame` callback, so neither `requestAnimationFrame(animate)` (`:714`) nor `canvas.remove()` (`:715`) runs — the animation freezes ~1.1s in while bursts 2–5 are still bright, and the `position:fixed; z-index:10000` canvas appended to `document.body` is never removed, because `renderFillOut()` does not touch `document.body`. A frozen spray of confetti stays painted over the app until a page reload. `pointer-events:none` means taps still work. Fired from `:3171`, the `allDone` submit-success branch — the crew's most common success path — and each occurrence ships an ERROR-level entry to the server via `log.js`, polluting production error logs. Pre-existing, not branch-caused (`dev` carries the same code at `:673`/`:3155`). **Destination: next night, with B-89 (T-34 decision 137)** (morning triage 2026-08-03, found by adversarial reproduction of run `overnight-20260804`)
