B-137 [done — reframed first-person as docs/contracts/NOTICE-sales-processor-2026-09-01.md + owner lines added to both contract docs (Card 10); finalised 2026-09-01 (ledger T-48)] Two ledger decisions and three runs' drafting treated sales-processor as an external counterparty; the operator maintains it themselves — established by asking directly at morning triage 2026-08-03. Nothing in this repo ever recorded ownership: `docs/contracts/inventory-period-summary.md:3` says only *"hand this document to the sales-processor maintainer"* and `:5` that the work was kept in its own repo *"per the developer's decision"* — and the developer **is** the operator. The repo is not on this box (`/home/jcole/projects/` holds hq, night-crew, slack-trading, infra, ui-jury, claude-skills and nothing else), which reads as "external" and is really "not written yet". **Consequence:** `NOTICE-sales-processor-2026-08-03-UNSENT.md` is drafted as a second-person apology to an external maintainer, asking questions only that maintainer could answer; ledger **decision 106** (two notices, sent separately, June drift first and alone) and **decision 128** (amending it to one combined notice) both reasoned entirely about sequencing outbound comms, and neither premise held. Cost: drafting across runs `20260803` and `20260804` plus a triage morning, and a P6 fix-forward checklist item (B4) spent re-framing a sequencing question that had no counterparty. 🛑 **What is NOT diminished:** the 111-row / 45-wrong audit stands (the contract documents are the spec sales-processor is built against), §1 is an operational finding about the operator's *own* payroll gate rather than a courtesy, and the coordinated-release constraint is unchanged — owning both sides removes the negotiation, not the one-hour disagreement. · _morning triage 2026-08-03_ (Destination: next milestone — record counterparty ownership in the contract documents themselves, so no future card infers "external" from "absent".)
B-138 [done — accounted for by the consumer's extended check; §1 closed, no restatement] The §1 "no missing weeks" evidence stops 16 days short, and two weekly periods are unaccounted for — the consumer closed the payroll-gate question on the strength of an unbroken weekly run for every period **2026-05-31 → 2026-07-19**, reasoning correctly that its gate hard-fails before writing a report or dispatching a transfer, so a spurious `ready:false` would show up as a **missing week on disk**. Today is **2026-08-04**: the periods ending **2026-07-26** and **2026-08-02** are outside the window it checked. By the reply's own detection method those two weeks are exactly where a spurious block would sit undetected — the test is sound, it just was not run over the most recent data. Not a contradiction of the consumer's evidence; a gap in its coverage, found by reading the reply rather than by the consumer. 🛑 Also worth one glance while checking: HQ's own `.env.prod` was last modified **2026-07-19**, the same date the reported runs stop. Probably coincidence — prod simply has not been redeployed since — but if the two weeks turn out to be missing, that timestamp is the first place to look. **✅ RESOLVED 2026-08-04 — the consumer extended the check and both weeks are accounted for.** Neither period has a run at **any** pipeline stage (classify, PDF, CSV, transfer ledger all stop at 07-19): nothing was blocked because **nothing ran** — both were operator skips, the business was closed. HQ's `/period-summary` returns `ready: true`, zero pending, zero unlinked for both periods. No spurious block in the extended window, no restatement. 🛑 **The consumer volunteered the finding that outlasts the answer:** *"a week we skip is byte-for-byte indistinguishable on disk from a week your gate blocks — both leave no report and no ledger."* So "unbroken weekly run ⇒ no blocks" was only **conditionally** valid and nobody had noticed the condition; it holds for 2026-05-31 → 2026-07-19 only because the first reply asserted every period in that window is *present*, not merely that present ones completed. The consumer is changing its process to run payroll even on closed weeks. HQ's half of the same blind spot is filed as **B-139**. (morning triage follow-up 2026-08-04, T-35 decision 142; resolved T-36 decision 146)
B-139 [promoted → pipeline-fail-loud] `/period-summary` logs no successful request and no `ready` verdict, so HQ cannot see a blocked payroll week at all — `internal/inventory/handler.go` carries ten `slog.Error` calls on this path and **not one log line for a successful response**. Nothing records that a request arrived, for which period, or what `completeness.ready` came back as; there is no access log on the service-token routes either (`internal/auth/*.go` has no logging). **Consequence:** the only record that a payroll week was blocked is an *absence* of a report on the consumer's disk — and the consumer established 2026-08-04 that an absence is byte-for-byte indistinguishable from a week it simply skipped. So the single most consequential answer this endpoint gives is the one nothing retains. HQ spent a triage morning and two rounds of correspondence reconstructing from the consumer's filesystem what one log line would have stated outright. 🛑 **Same class as B-81** (a reset that could not be seen in a gate log), **B-82**, **B-86**, **B-93** (a gate whose own summary was destroyed by a pipe) — the "a check cannot tell you what it actually did" cluster. The consumer is fixing its half by running payroll even on closed weeks; that is discipline, and this half is mechanism, which is the more durable of the two. **Destination: next milestone, with the B-81/B-82/B-86/B-93 cluster.** (morning triage follow-up 2026-08-04, found while recording the consumer's reply (T-36 decision 147))
B-140 [promoted → sync-doc-honesty] The stale `sync-rxdb-row-visibility-rls` activation gate survives at four more sites five days after that card merged, one of them inside `sync-rxdb/` and one in a shipped precached asset — the card merged **2026-08-01** at `bbbfc64` (run `overnight-20260801`; roadmap flipped it DONE at `914536c`), and four live comments still name it as a precondition that has not landed: `sync-rxdb/conflict-notice-ui.js:26` (*"the /sync door answers 503 by design until row-visibility RLS lands"*), `workflows.html:3560` (*"answers 503 by design until `sync-rxdb-row-visibility-rls` lands"* — the only survivor that spells the slug), `workflows.html:329` (*"must not happen until row-visibility RLS lands"*), and `tests/states-sync-rxdb-conflict-notice.spec.js:30` (same paraphrase). Card `w0-repo-hygiene` retired the two it owned — `sync-rxdb/bootstrap.js:22` and `sync-rxdb/client.js:1106-1111` — and its merge-intent note then claimed the directory was clean ("both sites, not one"); `conflict-notice-ui.js` makes that false, so there were **five** sites, not two. **Consequence:** a later author reads a merged card as an open precondition and either waits for work that already shipped or re-does it. The genuinely open precondition is the **cutover itself** — no page calls `startHQReplication`, so setting `HQ_SYNC_REST_URL` today would start a replication nothing reads — and a gate naming the wrong card hides that one. 🛑 **Also, and this is the more useful half: `tests/repo-hygiene.spec.js`'s case 3 (`no sync-rxdb source gates activation on the merged row-visibility-rls card`) is spelling-shaped, not fact-shaped.** It scans only `sync-rxdb/*.js` for the literal string `sync-rxdb-row-visibility-rls`, so it is blind to every paraphrase and to the whole tree outside that directory — which is exactly why it passes today with four live gates standing, three of them in files it never opens. **Demonstrated, not theorised:** re-inserting a paraphrased gate ("until row-visibility RLS lands", no slug) into `bootstrap.js` left the suite at **4 passed**. A guard that can be defeated by rewording the sentence it guards is a guard against typing, not against the defect. **Why this is a card and not a triage edit:** `workflows.html:3560` sits in a precached shipped asset, so retiring it forces an `sw.js` regeneration (B-13 — an `sw.js` you did not commit does not deploy) and therefore a full-suite gate; that is a night's cost, not a same-footprint tidy-up. **Destination: next milestone — with the sync cutover work, since `sync-hard-cutover` rewrites `workflows.html:3560`'s surrounding block anyway.** (found by G6 adversarial review on card `w0-repo-hygiene`, run `20260806`)
B-141 [promoted → gate-rls-fixture-ownership (prefix guard) + test-cluster-separation (structural half; ledger §T-38 decision 155)] 🛑 A destructive-DDL parameter is guarded by a four-item literal blocklist, and the list cannot contain the names that matter — this destroyed the production database on 2026-08-06 — `backend/internal/sync/rowvisibility_rls_test.go` takes `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB` from the environment and feeds it, unquoted, into `DROP DATABASE <name> WITH (FORCE)` against `defaultHQAdminURL = postgres://yumyums:yumyums@localhost:5433/postgres` — **which is the production cluster on this box**. Card `a3-rls-fixture-own` (run `20260806`) correctly removed the old shared *default* and then guarded the now-mandatory environment value with a blocklist of four literals: `hq_test_b2_fdw`, `hq_test_go`, `hq_test_e2e`, `postgres`. **Measured, one name per invocation:** `yumyums` → **accepted, dropped and recreated production**; `hq_test_g6a3_0806_go` (the running leg's own `DB_TEST_URL` database) → accepted, dropped; `hq_rls_a1_0806` (another card's live isolation name) → accepted, dropped. The card's own source comment recognises the exposure — *"the environment is now the ONLY source of an identifier that goes into destructive DDL"* — and then chooses the one shape that cannot be complete, because the dangerous names are **per-leg and per-environment** and therefore unknowable at authoring time. Two of the three are available to the process at runtime and free (`DB_TEST_URL`'s database, `TEST_DB_NAME`); the application database is in `backend/.env`/`DB_URL`. 🛑 **Aggravating:** the same card makes supplying the name **mandatory for every leg**, so it multiplies the number of times a human or an agent hand-types a database name straight into `DROP DATABASE … WITH (FORCE)` against the production cluster. **Consequence, realised:** during G6 adversarial review of that very card, a probe of the blocklist set `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB=yumyums`; the suite accepted it and dropped the production database. The `production` schema was destroyed with **no PITR (`archive_mode=off`), no dump on the box, and an empty alternate volume** — see B-143. Prod was rebuilt structurally empty by operator decision the same night. **This defect outlives card A3 and must be filed regardless of that card's disposition.** (found by G6 adversarial review on card `a3-rls-fixture-own`, run `20260806`, by realising it)
B-142 [promoted → gate-rls-fixture-ownership (attended re-gate; ledger §T-38 decision 155)] Card `a3-rls-fixture-own`'s own new test blind-drops the exact database B-35 is about, and the card breaks `task test:go` — held here because the card is **NOT merged** (G6 verdict DO NOT MERGE) and its branch `card/a3-rls-fixture-own` must not be the only record of what it still owes. Two outstanding fixes. **(a)** `rowvisibility_rls_test.go:2658-2665`: `dropVictim()` runs unconditionally **before** `CREATE DATABASE` and again in `t.Cleanup`, and `TestRVFixtureDatabase_AnUnnamedRunEatsNothing` never calls `rvConnect`, so it **never resolves `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB`** and cannot tell a database it created from one it found. Measured: a fully compliant leg (`HQ_RLS_TEST_DB=hq_rls_g6a3_0806`) destroyed `hq_test_b2_fdw` — a database it did not create and did not name — and left it dropped, `EXIT=0`. That is precisely the property the card exists to remove, reproduced inside the card. It matters during the transition window: every sibling worktree on this box is on a pre-A3 tree and still *defaults* to that name. The red-first design genuinely needs that name, so the fix is **not** to rename the victim but to refuse to run when it already exists — `t.Fatalf("someone is holding it")` — instead of dropping blind. **(b)** `Taskfile.yml:101` and `:132` both run `cd backend && go test -p 1 ./...` with **no `HQ_RLS_TEST_DB`**, so on merge `task test:go` and `task test:all` — the repo's two documented test targets, and the gate CLAUDE.md names — fail for every user on the first sync test. One `env:` line each. The card's merge-intent covers "any `go test ./...` a human types tomorrow" generically but never names the Taskfile, and lists what it did *not* touch as a virtue without noting that `Taskfile.yml` now *needs* touching. (found by G6 adversarial review on card `a3-rls-fixture-own`, run `20260806`)
B-143 [promoted → prod-backup-floor-and-pitr (ledger §T-38 decision 154)] 🛑 The production database has no backup of any kind, and this was discovered by needing one — verified on 2026-08-06 while attempting recovery: `archive_mode=off` and `archive_command=(disabled)` on the `yumyums-dev-pg` cluster, so **no PITR**; no `pg_dump`/backup target in `Taskfile.yml` or any compose file; no `*.sql`/`*.dump`/`*.sql.gz` anywhere under `/home/jcole` or `/mnt/c/Users/jcole`; and the second volume `yumyums-pgdata` (created 2026-07-14) is **empty** (4.0K, verified read-only) while the live volume is `yumyums_yumyums-pgdata`. **Consequence, realised:** when the production database was dropped (B-141), there was nothing to restore from, and the only available action was to rebuild it structurally empty. Data re-derivable from upstream systems — Mercury receipts, DO Spaces, Toast sales — survives as a re-import problem. Data that existed **only** in Postgres is gone: workflow templates, submissions and responses, real users and sessions, stock count overrides, recipes. 🛑 **This is the finding that converted a bad night into an unrecoverable one.** B-141 is the proximate cause; this is why it was terminal. `task prod:deploy` documents a `prod:rollback` for the *image* and nothing at all for the *data*, which is exactly the asymmetry that made the image reversible and the database not. (realised during recovery, run `20260806`)
B-145 [**per-class status:** purchases + receipts **restored** — backfill to 2026-03-01 ran clean (149 processed, 58 auto-created events, 84 in pending review for the crew, 0 errors; a Mercury pagination defect blocked the first attempt and was fixed with tests — `internal/receipt/mercury.go`, offset→cursor, plus new `cmd/backfill-receipts`); item catalog + vendors **restored via the backfill** (263 items, 20 vendors); Toast menu + daily sales **restored** (34 menu items, 1 052 sales rows, 80 business days 2026-03-05→08-02; Spaces archive first made gapless through 20260805 from the SFTP tail); users **partially rebuilt** (superadmin live; crew re-invites are the operator's, open); recipes **open — operator hand-rebuild** in the Recipes tab, COGS attribution wrong until done; submissions + responses, stock count overrides, sessions, and everything outside the inventory app **written off** (decision 157, operator's explicit scope); sales-processor notice **held by the operator** (decision 158, no draft). Backup floor + PITR landed BEFORE reconstruction (decision 154 both halves: nightly dump/globals/basebackup at 03:30 proven by observed firing, WAL archiving live outside the pgdata volume, restore drill green)] 🛑 Production is running structurally empty — decide, per data class, what gets re-imported, what gets reconstructed, and what is accepted as lost, then execute it — *As the owner whose crew uses this console daily, I want each class of the destroyed production data either restored, rebuilt, or explicitly written off, so that the business is not silently operating on an empty database while the gap compounds.* Follows directly from **B-141** (the drop) and **B-143** (nothing to restore from): after the 2026-08-06 incident, prod was rebuilt structurally empty by operator decision the same night, and nothing since has put data back. The classes split by where truth survives. **Re-derivable from upstream, a re-import problem:** Mercury receipts (and whatever the DO Spaces bucket still holds — uploaded receipt files live outside Postgres and should have survived; verify before assuming), Toast menu items and daily sales. Each needs its retention window measured *soon* — re-import gets harder or impossible as upstream windows close, which is what makes this urgent rather than merely important. **Existed only in Postgres, gone:** workflow templates, submissions and responses, real users and sessions, stock count overrides, recipes (`usage_pct` allocations). Templates, users and recipes are reconstructable by hand at known cost (templates re-authored in the Builder, users re-invited, recipe sliders re-set); submissions/responses and stock overrides are history and likely write-offs — but that is the operator's call to make explicitly, not a default. 🛑 **Two live consequences while this sits open:** the crew re-enters operating state by hand into an empty console, and the sales-processor contract endpoints (`period-summary` for weekly payroll COGS, `menu-cogs` for the weekly report) compute over a purchase history with a hole in it — the current payroll period's COGS is wrong until purchases are re-imported or the gap is declared to sales-processor's owner. **Decision and execution split:** the per-class rulings (restore / rebuild / write off, and what to tell sales-processor) are attended and the operator's own; the mechanical legs — a Mercury/Spaces backfill through the existing receipt pipeline, a Toast re-sync — are cardable once ruled. Sequencing note: `prod-backup-floor-and-pitr` (decision 154's card) should land **before** hours of manual reconstruction go in, so the rebuilt data is itself protected from day one. · _operator-directed filing, 2026-08-06, from HANDOFF run `20260806` next-action 1_ (**executed 2026-08-06** (attended; rulings = ledger T-39 decisions 156–159; handoff `reference/handoff-prod-data-recovery-20260806.md`))
B-146 [promoted → toast-ingest-resurrection] Prod's Toast sync has been silently dead since the 2026-07-28 image rebuild — the SFTP key never ships — `TOAST_SFTP_KEY_PATH=./id_rsa` resolves inside the container to `/app/id_rsa`, and that file does not exist there: the key lives only in the dev repo at `backend/id_rsa` (git-ignored, so the prod clone never receives it via `git reset --hard origin/main`), the image build does not COPY it, and `docker inspect yumyums-prod` shows **zero mounts**. Measured 2026-08-06 during B-145 Phase 0: the Spaces Toast archive's last date-directory is `toast/20260724` (last write 2026-07-26) and the container was built 2026-07-28 — sync worked until the rebuild replaced an image that evidently had the key, then stopped without any alert. Toast's SFTP side retains a ~27-day rolling window (measured: `20260710`–`20260805`), so each silent week burns real retention margin; the Spaces archive currently covers `20260305`–`20260724` and together they are still gapless, but only because this was caught inside the window. Fix shape: put the key beside `.env.prod` in the prod clone as an untracked file and bind-mount or COPY it in (the `.env.prod` secrets pattern already survives the hard reset), and give the sync loop a fail-loud path — a sync that cannot even open SFTP should surface in `/api/v1/health` or the Cliq alert, not log-and-continue. (found during B-145 recovery Phase 0, 2026-08-06)
