B-161 [**done — answered GREEN by card E, run `20260808` (T-42)**: a severed client recovered all three dark-window changes including the in-place UPDATE, via checkpoint pull riding the trigger-stamped substrate `_modified` (strict `gt` + id tie-breaker, minted at the projection boundary independent of any HQ business timestamp) — the feared `gte.<iso>` weak spot does not exist on this path. The green is CONDITIONAL on the relay staying trigger/NOTIFY-driven: a polling relay on a business watermark reintroduces the miss (`submitted_at` measured never advancing after INSERT — a creation timestamp wearing a watermark's name). Red `--no-pull` exit 1 proved the assertion set non-vacuous; both legs re-executed live at triage with teardown byte-verified] The disconnect/reconnect/catch-up recovery story is architecture-implied but proven by nothing — spikes A–D proved the filter semantics (D) and the write→read round trip (C), but no test anywhere disconnects a replicating client, writes rows while it is dark, reconnects it, and asserts the rows arrive. The claim "a reconnecting client pulls forward from its checkpoint and re-reads state" rides on RxDB's replication protocol doing what its docs say against THIS substrate with THIS checkpoint shape — and the checkpoint clause is `submitted_at=gte.<iso>`, so a row *updated* without `submitted_at` advancing is exactly the kind of change a catch-up pull could miss (the same unreliable-watermark concern that disqualified `answered_at` for the polling relay). An ignored catch-up is the quiet failure shape this repo keeps re-finding: the system behaves identically until a crew member's phone sleeps through a write. · _operator question, post-triage session 2026-08-07_ (promoted → spike-e-reconnect-catchup)
B-163 [done — all four (a–d) landed on `card/c2-spike-exit-code-honesty`, red-first proven 1→2 per path] Spike-E harness maps infra failures to RED and its `task` wrapper lies about the exit code — three latent exit-code conflations, all confirmed by execution-adjacent reading at triage (none fired in any recorded run): (a) unguarded `$(srcpsql …)` assignments at `spike-e-reconnect.sh:411,463-464,552-554` under `set -euo pipefail` die with psql's own exit code — an infra failure can read as RED (exit 1), which would falsely instruct Activity 3 to add a resync step; (b) `:564` — `CLIENT_RC=0` with `B_ROWS≠1` calls `red` where the contract's honest code for "green was vacuous, no verdict" is 2; (c) an uncaught exception outside the try blocks in `rxdb/spike-e-reconnect.js` exits Node 1 → RED. Plus: `task spike:reconnect:red` exits **201** (go-task's failing-command code), not the script's 1 — anyone gating on the wrapper misreads the RED-FIRST capture; gate on the script directly. **✅ RESOLVED 2026-08-09 on run `20260809`, branch `card/c2-spike-exit-code-honesty` (card C2).** (a) The seven bare `VAR="$(srcpsql …)"` assignments that die under `set -euo pipefail` with psql/docker's own code (`TABLES`, `HQ_USER_ID`, `SUBMIT_TRG`, `RESP_TRG`, `B_ROWS`, `A_ROWS`, `C_ROWS` — a superset of the four cited; the printf-arg and `mapfile < <(…)` substitutions do NOT die under `set -e` and are gated by their own count checks, left unchanged) now carry `|| cannot_run …` → exit 2. (b) The `CLIENT_RC=0` + `B_ROWS≠1` branch (was `:564`, now `:591`) calls `cannot_run` (exit 2 — vacuous green, no verdict), not `red`. (c) `rxdb/spike-e-reconnect.js` installs top-level `uncaughtException`/`unhandledRejection` handlers mapping any escape from the unprotected main flow to `die(SETUP)` → exit 2. (d) `Taskfile.yml` `spike:reconnect:red` now carries a comment note on the 201 trap (gate on the script, not `task`). Red-first proven for each of (a)/(b)/(c) with deliberately-missing-coordinate probes (each exit 1 → exit 2), captured in the run's merge-intent. Note: like B-148, the full live spike was NOT re-run — B-163 states the recorded verdict does not depend on any of these; the fixes are execution-verified via exit-code probes and static/`node --check` review. (run `20260808` G6 findings 2–4 + triage adversarial review, morning triage 2026-08-07; resolved run `20260809` card C2)
